.TH IOSTATS 4
.SH NAME
iostats \- file system to measure I/O
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iostats
[
.B -d
]
[
.B -t
.I tracefile
]
[
.B -f
.I dbfile
]
.I cmd
[
.I args...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Iostats
is a file server that interposes itself between a program and a copy of the current name space,
which allows it to gather statistics of file system
use at the level of the file system protocol of
.IR intro (5).
When the program exits,
a report is printed on standard output.
.PP
The report consists of three sections.
The first section reports the amount
of user data in
.B read
and
.B write
messages sent by the program and the average rate at
which the data was transferred.
The
.B protocol
line reports the amount
of data sent as message headers, that is,
protocol overhead.
The
.B rpc
line reports the
total number of file system transactions.
.PP
The second section gives
the number of messages, the fastest, slowest, and average turn around
time and the amount of data involved with each 9P
message type.
The final section gives an I/O summary for each file used
by the program in terms of opens, reads and writes.
.PP
The
.B -d
option causes
.I iostats
to write a debugging log to
.I dbfile
(default:
.BR iostats.out )
that records all protocol messages.
.SH EXAMPLE
Display summary of file I/O incurred by
.IR ls (1):
.IP
.EX
iostats ls
.EE
.PP
Start a new shell, displaying all 9P traffic caused by the shell or its children:
.IP
.EX
iostats -df /fd/1 sh
.EE
.SS "Recording for replay"
.B -t
writes every message to
.I tracefile
exactly as it crossed the wire, with the time at which it crossed.
.PP
This is not what
.B -d
produces.
That prints a readable rendering, and a rendering cannot be replayed: a
read appears as
.RB \(lq "array[2167] of byte" \(rq
without the bytes, and no line carries a time.
.PP
Each record is
.IP
.EX
\&'T' or 'R'   1 byte, the direction
time         8 bytes, big-endian microseconds
length       4 bytes, big-endian
message      length bytes, the Styx message verbatim
.EE
.PP
The two directions are read by separate processes which hand records to
one writer, so the file is a total order over both and no descriptor
offset is shared.
.PP
The times come from
.BR /dev/time ,
which reports microseconds despite the internal function being called
.BR nsec .
They are good enough to reproduce pacing at human scale: a script that
read a file, slept a second, read another, slept two and read a third
produced a trace spanning 3.031s in which the two pauses are recovered
as 1.005s and 2.005s.
.SS "What a trace does and does not determine"
Because devices in Inferno are files, a great deal that is usually
outside a trace is inside this one: pointer and keyboard input, the draw
protocol, anything under
.BR /chan ,
and reads of
.B /dev/time
and
.BR /dev/random .
.PP
Two things escape it.
.B Sys->millisec
and
.B Sys->sleep
are system calls rather than files
.RI ( emu/port/inferno.c ),
so a program that paces itself with those is not fully captured; one that
reads
.B /dev/time
is.
And the order in which a program's own processes run is not recorded.
.PP
That second gap is smaller here than it would be elsewhere.
Dis execution is serialised \(em exactly one process executes
instructions at a time, see
.IR refstress (1)
\(em so the schedule is a sequence of discrete handoffs rather than an
arbitrary interleaving, which is a far smaller thing to record than it
would be on a machine that ran them side by side.
.SS "What a program can do to escape it"
The interposition is on the name space, so what is captured is what the
program reaches
.I through
the name space \(em and a program that attaches a kernel device itself
does not.
.B Bind
of
.BR "#i" ,
.BR "#m" ,
.B "#c"
and the rest goes straight to the device, past anything mounted on the
way, and none of the traffic appears.
.PP
This is not hypothetical and is easy to meet by accident, because it is
what a graphics program normally does.
.IR Drawbind (1)
exists to show it: one program that allocates a display and an image
through
.IR draw (2),
differing only in whether it binds
.B "#i"
onto
.B /dev
itself or inherits a
.B /dev
its parent bound.
.IP
.EX
% bind -b '#i' /dev
% iostats -t /a.styx drawbind
% iostats -t /b.styx drawbind -b
.EE
.PP
Both allocate the image and say so.
The traces do not agree that they did:
.IP
.EX
% styxlog -f draw /a.styx
    0.023  open   /dev/draw/new
    0.023  open   /dev/draw/1/data
    0.023  open   /dev/draw/1/refresh
    0.024  write  /dev/draw/1/data (51 bytes)
    ...
% styxlog -f draw /b.styx
%
.EE
.PP
Not one message, though the work was done and succeeded.
That is the point of running the same program both ways: a trace with
nothing in it otherwise looks like a program that did nothing.
.PP
So bind the devices in the shell that runs
.BR iostats ,
not in the program being traced.
An inherited name space is traced; a self-attached device is not.
.SS "The report"
It goes to standard
.IR output ,
not standard error as this page used to say, which is easy to miss when
a traced program's own output is being captured.
.PP
Its times are microseconds.
They were being divided by a million and labelled milliseconds, which
made every line read 0 ms, and by 1.0e9 to make a rate, which claimed
6860585 Kb/sec for reading a twenty-kilobyte file.
Both came from
.B /dev/time
reporting microseconds while the function that reads it is called
.BR nsec .
.PP
.IR Styxreplay (1)
serves a trace back to a program.
.IR Styxlog (1)
reads one, and reports what the session did as opens and writes against
paths rather than as messages about fids.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/iostats.b
.SH BUGS
Poor clock resolution means that large amounts of I/O must be done to
get accurate rate figures.
.PP
Can be fooled by programs that do fresh mounts outside its purview, and
by any program that binds a kernel device itself; see
.I "What a program can do to escape it"
above.
.PP
Cannot monitor standard input or output.
